SECTION 1. PRODUCT OVERVIEW
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Name: EcoTriage
Tagline: "Keep Emergency Routes Moving. Even When Networks Go Dark."
Home headline: "Help reaches blocked roads faster."
Home sub-line: "Report hazards. Find help. Stay safe."
Subtitle under the logo: "Emergency Hazard Intelligence"
City: Visakhapatnam, Andhra Pradesh, India. Team: Webuzz.

The problem: after cyclones, municipal authorities lose critical hours finding and ranking blocked roads, because reports arrive through scattered phone calls and messaging apps. Blocked roads delay rescue, medical transport and power restoration.

The solution: a web platform with two sides.
(a) Operations side: field officers and clearance squads report blockages with a photo and GPS. An AI model reads the photo and gives a priority score. Command officers see a live, colour-coded, priority-ranked map and queue, dispatch crews, and track progress.
(b) Public-help side: anyone can find nearby relief camps, hospitals and support services, read safety precautions, read about past cyclones, and see the people who helped (Our Heroes).
The platform keeps working when the network is down: reports are saved on the device and sent when the network returns.

Browser only. No native Android or iOS app. It must work well on a 360px phone browser and on a 1440px laptop browser.

Do NOT build: drone integration, native app-store apps, payment or billing, pre-storm tree-health tracking.
Do NOT use any government emblem, national flag graphic, real government agency logo, or real corporation name (use generic names such as "Municipal Clearance Unit" and "State Electricity Board (sample)"). Label every piece of demo content "Sample".

THE ONE MAIN ACTION
"Report Hazard": take or upload a photo, the app tags GPS, the AI scores the hazard, and it appears on the live map and in the queue. This is a solid red button, always visible (in the header on desktop, as a floating button and in the bottom bar on mobile). A hazard can be reported in 2 taps or fewer from any page.

